; RadioTrail.inix -- how this database opens, and how each table is heard.
;
; INTERNET RADIO, AS A LIST YOU CAN SEARCH AND SORT. One table, stations, holds
; a station's name, its stream address, where it is from, its language, its
; genres, its codec and bitrate, and how listeners rate it. Fourteen stations
; are here to start: the ten ACB Media streams, the NFB Radio Network, Radio
; Paradise and two SomaFM channels. Run fetchStations, in this folder, to bring
; in the whole Radio Browser catalog -- some sixty thousand working stations --
; and all forty-six SomaFM channels, or one country of Radio Browser.
;
; LISTENING IS ONE KEY. On a station's row, move to the stream_url column and
; press Control+Enter, Open Cell Value: Windows hands the address to whatever
; plays streams on your computer, as VLC does. Shift+C says the cell.
;
; WHAT A STATION SAYS ABOUT ITSELF is not in the catalog: "Official home of
; the Seattle Seahawks" lives in the stream's own headers and on the station's
; home page. fetchStations --enrich asks each station once and files the
; answer -- the slogan in slogan, the rest in descrip, new genre words in
; genre -- so Keywords finds Seahawks. fetchStations does this as its third
; step; --country "United States" keeps the whole run to one country.
;
; WHERE IT COMES FROM. The stations: Radio Browser, a volunteer directory, and
; SomaFM's channel list; the official records: Wikipedia. The design, and the
; ACB Media and NFB Radio addresses, follow Quill Radio, the successor to the
; ACB Radio Tuner, whose published requirements and source were studied here.
;
; WHAT IS YOURS STAYS YOURS. status (favorite, tried, dead, want to try),
; rating, notes and tags are the listener's fields; a refresh from the catalog
; never writes them, and the merge is keyed by the catalog's own id for each
; station, never by the stream address, which real stations share.

[Database]

InitialTable = stations

[Table:stations]

; Name first, so a letter reaches the station; then what it plays and where it
; is. State is not on the row: it is empty for most of the world, and an empty
; cell is a "blank" spoken on every row. Within one country, Select Columns
; adds it. Filter Records on genre, country, state or language is the way
; through sixty thousand rows; Keywords searches every field, on screen or
; off -- slogan, descrip, homepage, codec, all of them.
;
; FILLING IT IS ONE COMMAND: fetchStations, in this folder. A clean copy, the
; whole catalog, then every station asked what it says about itself -- an
; hour or two, which can be stopped and picked up again -- and then, for a
; station with a broadcast call sign in its name, its official record from
; Wikipedia: call sign, frequency, city of licence, owner and format.
;
; WHERE EACH THING GOES. A fact most stations have is a column. Words that
; vary -- the catalog's tags, a stream's genre words, a page's keywords, the
; article's format -- are in tags, one per line. Prose is in notes: what the
; station says of itself, and the article's opening paragraph. Both only ever
; grow; a line you add stays. status and rating are yours alone.
SelectFields = name, genre, country
OrderFields = name
WhereFilter =

[Table:lookups]

SelectFields = fld, val
OrderFields = fld, ordinal
WhereFilter =

[Table:maps]

SelectFields = kind, prime1, prime2
OrderFields = kind, prime1
WhereFilter =
